window.CH14_LESSON_CARDS=[{"id":"ch14-lesson-001","term":"Abnormal behavior is context-dependent","type":"lesson-concept","category":"Psychological disorder basics","cue":"Think: culture and context matter","simple":"Psychologists do not define abnormal behavior by a single culture-free rule; they consider deviation, distress, harm, and cultural context.","examples":["A behavior that is unusual in one culture may be accepted in another.","The chapter warns that simply being statistically uncommon does not automatically make behavior a disorder."],"apply":["A behavior is rare but harmless and culturally accepted. Why is rarity alone not enough to call it abnormal?"],"compare":"Statistical rarity describes how uncommon a behavior is; a psychological disorder also involves clinically significant disturbance, distress, dysfunction, or harm."},{"id":"ch14-lesson-002","term":"Statistical approach to abnormal behavior","type":"lesson-concept","category":"Psychological disorder basics","cue":"Think: uncommon = statistically abnormal","simple":"The statistical approach treats behaviors shown by a minority of people as abnormal because they deviate from average patterns.","examples":["A behavior far outside the population average would be labeled abnormal by the statistical approach.","The chapter notes this approach can ignore distress and cultural differences."],"apply":["A psychologist labels a behavior abnormal because very few people in the population do it. Which approach is being used?"]},{"id":"ch14-lesson-003","term":"Distress and harm criteria","type":"lesson-concept","category":"Psychological disorder basics","cue":"Think: distressing or harmful","simple":"The chapter combines several approaches by emphasizing abnormal behavior that is distressing and harmful to the self or others.","examples":["Behavior that causes disabling distress is more clinically meaningful than behavior that is merely unusual.","Harm to others can be relevant except when someone is acting defensively."],"apply":["A behavior is common enough statistically but causes severe distress and major impairment. Why can it still be considered abnormal?"]},{"id":"ch14-lesson-004","term":"Signs vs. symptoms","type":"lesson-concept","category":"Psychological disorder basics","cue":"Think: observed vs. reported","simple":"Signs are objective behaviors observed by a clinician; symptoms are experiences or problems described by the client.","examples":["A clinician observes slowed movement: that is a sign.","A client reports persistent hopelessness: that is a symptom."],"apply":["A therapist sees a behavior directly, while the client reports an internal experience. Which is the sign and which is the symptom?"]},{"id":"ch14-lesson-005","term":"DSM and ICD in diagnosis","type":"lesson-concept","category":"Psychological disorder basics","cue":"Think: U.S. usually uses DSM","simple":"Mental-health professionals use classification systems such as the DSM and the ICD; the chapter states that U.S. professionals most often rely on the DSM.","examples":["The DSM is published by the American Psychiatric Association.","The ICD is published by the World Health Organization."],"apply":["A clinician in the United States needs the classification resource most commonly used there. Which system should you expect them to consult?"]},{"id":"ch14-lesson-006","term":"Biopsychosociocultural model","type":"lesson-concept","category":"Psychological disorder basics","cue":"Think: biology + psychology + social + culture","simple":"Psychological disorders are best understood as the result of interacting biological, psychological, social, and cultural influences.","examples":["A genetic predisposition can interact with poor coping skills, chronic stress, and social isolation.","The model rejects explanations that rely on only one type of cause."],"apply":["A case is explained using genes, coping style, family support, and cultural expectations together. Which model is being used?"]},{"id":"ch14-lesson-007","term":"ASD vs. ADHD pattern","type":"lesson-concept","category":"Childhood disorders","cue":"Think: social-communication/repetition vs. attention/hyperactivity","simple":"ASD centers on social-communication difficulties and repetitive or restricted behavior, while ADHD centers on developmentally unusual inattention and/or hyperactive-impulsive behavior.","examples":["Difficulty sustaining attention and frequent fidgeting fit ADHD.","Poor back-and-forth social communication and highly restricted interests fit ASD."],"apply":["A child fails to respond to social cues and has repetitive restricted interests. Which side of the ASD-versus-ADHD distinction fits best?"]},{"id":"ch14-lesson-008","term":"Schizophrenia symptom pattern","type":"lesson-concept","category":"Schizophrenia","cue":"Think: delusions + hallucinations + disorganization + negative symptoms","simple":"Schizophrenia can include delusions, hallucinations, disorganized thought or speech, abnormal motor behavior, restricted affect, avolition, and asociality.","examples":["The chapter emphasizes delusions, hallucinations, disorganized speech, and grossly disorganized or abnormal motor behavior as major symptoms.","Schizophrenia is not the same as dissociative identity disorder."],"apply":["A person has hallucinations, delusions, disorganized speech, and very reduced motivation. What disorder pattern does this combination suggest?"]},{"id":"ch14-lesson-009","term":"Types of delusions","type":"lesson-concept","category":"Schizophrenia","cue":"Think: paranoia, grandiosity, control","simple":"Common delusions described in the chapter include persecution or paranoia, grandiosity, and beliefs that outside forces are controlling one's behavior.","examples":["Believing the CIA is trying to poison you is a persecutory or paranoid delusion.","Believing you have extraordinary power or a special relationship with a celebrity is grandiosity."],"apply":["A person falsely believes they are extraordinarily powerful and personally connected to a famous athlete. Which type of delusion is this?"]},{"id":"ch14-lesson-010","term":"Hallucinations vs. delusions","type":"lesson-concept","category":"Schizophrenia","cue":"Think: false perception vs. false belief","simple":"A hallucination is a false sensory perception; a delusion is a false, illogical belief. Auditory hallucinations are especially common in schizophrenia.","examples":["Hearing a voice with no external speaker is a hallucination.","Believing others are secretly persecuting you is a delusion."],"apply":["A person hears a voice that is not present. Is that a hallucination or a delusion, and why?"]},{"id":"ch14-lesson-011","term":"Disorganized speech","type":"lesson-concept","category":"Schizophrenia","cue":"Think: disjointed, incoherent thought expressed in speech","simple":"Disorganized speech reflects disjointed or incoherent thought and may involve invented words, rapid topic shifts, or repeated words and statements.","examples":["A person jumps rapidly between unrelated topics so that their speech is difficult to follow.","A person repeatedly uses made-up words that only they understand."],"apply":["Speech rapidly shifts topics and becomes incoherent. Which schizophrenia-related symptom does this illustrate?"]},{"id":"ch14-lesson-012","term":"Catatonia","type":"lesson-concept","category":"Schizophrenia","cue":"Think: abnormal motor behavior","simple":"Catatonia is a pattern of markedly abnormal motor behavior that can include mutism, unusual postures, or bursts of activity.","examples":["The chapter quiz pairs mutism and odd postures with catatonia."],"apply":["A person becomes mute and holds unusual postures for long periods. Which symptom pattern fits?"]},{"id":"ch14-lesson-013","term":"Schizophrenia and enlarged ventricles","type":"lesson-concept","category":"Schizophrenia","cue":"Think: biological correlate","simple":"The chapter quiz identifies enlarged brain ventricles as a biological finding reliably associated with schizophrenia.","examples":["Ventricles are fluid-filled spaces in the brain; the quiz uses their enlargement as a schizophrenia-associated biological finding."],"apply":["An exam item asks which disorder is associated with enlarged ventricles. Which disorder should you connect with that finding?"]},{"id":"ch14-lesson-014","term":"Bipolar disorder can resemble schizophrenia","type":"lesson-concept","category":"Mood disorders","cue":"Think: psychotic symptoms can overlap","simple":"Bipolar disorder may include psychotic symptoms such as hallucinations or delusions, which can lead to confusion with schizophrenia.","examples":["The chapter states that people with bipolar disorder who show psychotic symptoms may be incorrectly diagnosed with schizophrenia.","Bipolar disorder often also occurs with an anxiety disorder."],"apply":["Why can a person with bipolar disorder sometimes be mistaken for someone with schizophrenia?"]},{"id":"ch14-lesson-015","term":"Major depressive disorder diagnostic pattern","type":"lesson-concept","category":"Mood disorders","cue":"Think: at least 5 symptoms for at least 2 weeks","simple":"The chapter states that MDD requires at least five relevant symptoms for at least two weeks, representing a change from previous functioning, with depressed mood or loss of interest/pleasure among the key features.","examples":["Symptoms discussed include depressed mood, loss of pleasure, sleep/appetite disturbance, concentration difficulty, hopelessness, and possible suicidal thoughts.","Anhedonia means loss of interest or pleasure in activities that previously felt rewarding."],"apply":["A person has persistent low mood and loss of interest for only two days. Why does that not match the chapter's MDD duration criterion?"]},{"id":"ch14-lesson-016","term":"Cognitive explanation of depression","type":"lesson-concept","category":"Mood disorders","cue":"Think: negative views of self, world, and future","simple":"A cognitive explanation of depression emphasizes negative patterns of thinking about oneself, the world, and the future.","examples":["The chapter's 'Did You Get It?' question uses a therapist who explains depression through negative views of self, world, and future."],"apply":["A therapist focuses on a client's persistently negative beliefs about self, world, and future. Which theoretical explanation is being used?"]},{"id":"ch14-lesson-017","term":"Anterior cingulate and emotion regulation","type":"lesson-concept","category":"Mood disorders","cue":"Think: emotion regulation","simple":"The chapter identifies the anterior cingulate as an important brain structure in the regulation of emotion.","examples":["The MDD lesson's knowledge check asks which brain structure plays an important role in emotion regulation and identifies the anterior cingulate."],"apply":["Which brain structure should you associate with regulation of emotion in this chapter?"]},{"id":"ch14-lesson-018","term":"Core components of anxiety","type":"lesson-concept","category":"Anxiety, trauma, and somatic disorders","cue":"Think: negative emotion + physical tension + anticipation of danger","simple":"The chapter describes anxiety as involving strong negative emotions and physical tension arising from anticipation of danger.","examples":["Normal anxiety can be useful, but anxiety disorders involve anxiety that is disproportionate to circumstances."],"apply":["A person feels intense negative emotion and bodily tension because they expect danger. Which broad process is being described?"]},{"id":"ch14-lesson-019","term":"Panic attack vs. panic disorder vs. GAD","type":"lesson-concept","category":"Anxiety, trauma, and somatic disorders","cue":"Think: single attack vs. recurrent attacks vs. broad worry","simple":"A panic attack is a single episode of intense fear with autonomic arousal; panic disorder involves repeated attacks and fear of future attacks; GAD involves excessive persistent worry across many areas of life.","examples":["A 10-minute episode of pounding heart, sweating, trembling, chest pain, nausea, and dizziness fits a panic attack.","Persistent unexplained worry across many areas for months fits GAD."],"apply":["A person has one sudden 10-minute episode of intense fear and physical arousal. Is that enough by itself to establish panic disorder?"]},{"id":"ch14-lesson-020","term":"Obsession vs. compulsion","type":"lesson-concept","category":"Anxiety, trauma, and somatic disorders","cue":"Think: intrusive thought vs. repetitive act","simple":"An obsession is a recurrent intrusive unwanted thought; a compulsion is a repetitive behavior or mental act performed in response to distress or a rule.","examples":["Repeatedly thinking 'Did I lock the door?' is an obsession.","Repeatedly checking the lock to reduce anxiety is a compulsion."],"apply":["A person cannot stop an intrusive thought and then repeatedly checks the door. Identify the obsession and the compulsion."]},{"id":"ch14-lesson-021","term":"Hoarding disorder","type":"lesson-concept","category":"Anxiety, trauma, and somatic disorders","cue":"Think: difficulty discarding possessions","simple":"Hoarding disorder involves persistent difficulty discarding possessions, producing an accumulation that clutters living areas.","examples":["The lesson describes a person who keeps possessions because they may be useful later until the home becomes cluttered."],"apply":["A person's home becomes unusable because they cannot discard possessions they believe may be useful later. Which disorder fits?"]},{"id":"ch14-lesson-022","term":"Depersonalization","type":"lesson-concept","category":"Anxiety, trauma, and somatic disorders","cue":"Think: unreality or out-of-body feeling","simple":"Depersonalization involves strong feelings of unreality about oneself or the surrounding environment, including feeling detached from one's body or self.","examples":["The lesson describes an out-of-body experience in which a person feels detached from themselves."],"apply":["A person feels as though they are floating above their body and watching themselves from outside. Which dissociative experience fits?"]},{"id":"ch14-lesson-023","term":"Dissociative vs. somatic symptom disorders","type":"lesson-concept","category":"Anxiety, trauma, and somatic disorders","cue":"Think: identity/memory/consciousness vs. physical symptoms","simple":"Dissociative disorders involve disruptions in identity, memory, or consciousness; somatic symptom and related disorders center on physical symptoms.","examples":["The chapter contrasts memory or identity disruption with physical symptoms that may not have a clear medical explanation."],"apply":["A question asks whether a case is mainly about disrupted identity/memory or about physical symptoms. What distinction should you make?"]},{"id":"ch14-lesson-024","term":"Personality disorder distinctions","type":"lesson-concept","category":"Personality disorders","cue":"Think: ASPD vs. BPD vs. NPD","simple":"The chapter contrasts antisocial personality disorder (low remorse/empathy and disregard for rules), borderline personality disorder (unstable relationships, self-image, and emotion), and narcissistic personality disorder (grandiosity, need for admiration, low empathy).","examples":["Little regard for social rules and little remorse fits ASPD.","Severe instability in relationships and emotion fits BPD.","Entitlement and grandiosity fit NPD."],"apply":["A person believes they deserve special treatment and shows grandiosity with little empathy. Which personality-disorder pattern fits best?"]},{"id":"ch14-lesson-025","term":"Rule out medical causes before diagnosis","type":"lesson-concept","category":"Self-diagnosis and research","cue":"Think: medical problems can mimic psychological symptoms","simple":"Before diagnosing a psychological disorder, clinicians must consider and rule out medical conditions that could produce similar symptoms.","examples":["The self-diagnosis activity emphasizes that diagnosis can involve interviews, standardized assessments, questionnaires, and information from other people."],"apply":["Why should a clinician consider medical causes before concluding that symptoms reflect a psychological disorder?"]},{"id":"ch14-lesson-026","term":"Cross-sectional research","type":"lesson-concept","category":"Self-diagnosis and research","cue":"Think: one time point","simple":"A cross-sectional study collects data from participants at one point in time to examine relationships among variables.","examples":["The ChatGPT self-diagnosis study surveyed frequent users rather than randomly assigning them to conditions.","The study did not follow the same people over a long period."],"apply":["Researchers survey many people once to see whether variables are related. What research design is this?"]},{"id":"ch14-lesson-027","term":"ChatGPT self-diagnosis study finding","type":"lesson-concept","category":"Self-diagnosis and research","cue":"Think: perceived benefit → confidence → intended use","simple":"In the study summarized by the chapter, people who believed ChatGPT was effective and beneficial felt more confident in decisions after consulting it and were more likely to intend to use it for health-related self-diagnosis.","examples":["The activity's education table showed bachelor's-degree participants as the largest group among the reported ChatGPT users."],"apply":["According to the chapter's research activity, what belief about ChatGPT was associated with greater confidence and greater intended use for self-diagnosis?"]},{"apply":["A student claims psychological disorders are extremely rare. What statistic from the chapter corrects this?"],"category":"Psychological disorder basics","compare":"Lifetime prevalence across all disorders is about 1 in 2; a single disorder like schizophrenia is far rarer, about 1 in 100.","cue":"one in two","examples":["The chapter's opening video asks how many people live with a psychological disorder at some point — the answer is around one in two.","The lesson uses this statistic to show that psychological disorders are common, not rare."],"id":"ch14-lesson-028","simple":"about one in two people will experience a psychological disorder at some point in their lives","term":"Lifetime prevalence of psychological disorders","type":"lesson-concept"},{"apply":["Why does the chapter begin by testing what you know about mental health myths?"],"category":"Psychological disorder basics","compare":"Myths increase stigma and prejudice; accurate facts about disorders reduce them.","cue":"facts fight stigma","examples":["The chapter opens with a video on mental health myths because knowing fact from myth lowers stigma.","The lesson frames myth-busting as a way to reduce prejudice against people with disorders."],"id":"ch14-lesson-029","simple":"learning the facts about psychological disorders instead of believing myths reduces stigma and prejudice","term":"Mental health myths and stigma","type":"lesson-concept"},{"apply":["A culture values emotional restraint, and someone is very emotionally expressive. Why might the ideal approach mislabel this person?"],"category":"Psychological disorder basics","compare":"Ideal approach: abnormal means falling short of an ideal. Statistical approach: abnormal means being in the minority.","cue":"deviation from ideal","examples":["The lesson notes this approach captures the distress people feel when they engage in abnormal behavior.","The chapter warns that it fails to address cross-cultural differences in what counts as ideal."],"id":"ch14-lesson-030","simple":"the ideal approach calls behavior abnormal when it deviates from some ideal way of behaving","term":"Ideal approach to defining abnormal behavior","type":"lesson-concept"},{"apply":["Two people both injure someone, but one was defending herself from attack. How does the harm criterion treat them differently?"],"category":"Psychological disorder basics","compare":"Harm to others signals abnormality; harm done in self-defense does not.","cue":"harm, unless defending","examples":["The lesson states psychologists consider behavior that causes harm to others abnormal — with self-defense as the exception.","The chapter's quiz contrasts harmful behavior with behavior done in self-defense."],"id":"ch14-lesson-031","simple":"behavior that harms others counts as abnormal, except when the person is acting defensively","term":"Self-defense exception in the harm criterion","type":"lesson-concept"},{"apply":["A critic argues that an energetic but healthy child was diagnosed with ADHD. Which criticism of the DSM-5 is this?"],"category":"Psychological disorder basics","compare":"Supporters treat the DSM-5 as the standard US diagnostic tool; critics say it pathologizes normal behavior.","cue":"DSM overlabels kids","examples":["The lesson says critics believe this led to overdiagnosis of ADHD, autism, and childhood bipolar disorder.","The chapter notes most US professionals still rely on the DSM-5 despite the criticism."],"id":"ch14-lesson-032","simple":"critics say the DSM-5 labels some normal behaviors as disorders, causing overdiagnosis","term":"DSM-5 overdiagnosis criticism","type":"lesson-concept"},{"apply":["A researcher reports that 5% of children have ADHD. Which term describes this 5% figure?"],"category":"Psychological disorder basics","compare":"Prevalence is how common a disorder is; comorbidity is how often two disorders occur together.","cue":"how common is it","examples":["The lesson defines prevalence right after introducing comorbidity and familial disease.","The chapter gives prevalence figures like 1% for schizophrenia and 5% of children for ADHD."],"id":"ch14-lesson-033","simple":"prevalence is how frequently a disorder occurs in a population","term":"Prevalence","type":"lesson-concept"},{"apply":["Complete the definition: a psychological disorder is a ___ in an individual's cognition, emotion regulation, or behavior."],"category":"Psychological disorder basics","compare":"Clinically significant disturbance is the formal bar for a disorder; merely unconventional behavior does not clear it.","cue":"the formal definition","examples":["The chapter quiz defines a psychological disorder with this exact phrase.","The lesson's formal definition centers on disturbance plus underlying dysfunction, not just unusual behavior."],"id":"ch14-lesson-034","simple":"a psychological disorder is a clinically significant disturbance in cognition, emotion regulation, or behavior that reflects a dysfunction in mental functioning","term":"Clinically significant disturbance","type":"lesson-concept"},{"apply":["Someone has unusual hobbies but no distress or dysfunction. Why is this not automatically a disorder?"],"category":"Psychological disorder basics","compare":"Unconventional behavior is merely unusual; a disorder adds clinically significant disturbance, distress, or harm.","cue":"weird is not disordered","examples":["The lesson asks which thoughts and behaviors count as disorders versus merely unconventional ones.","The chapter stresses that psychologists look for distress, harm, and dysfunction — not just oddness."],"id":"ch14-lesson-035","simple":"behavior can be unconventional or idiosyncratic without being a psychological disorder","term":"Unconventional behavior vs. psychological disorder","type":"lesson-concept"},{"apply":["ASD and ADHD are both diagnosed in childhood and last into adulthood. What category do they share?"],"category":"Childhood disorders","compare":"Neurodevelopmental disorders begin in childhood; many other disorders, like schizophrenia, typically emerge later.","cue":"childhood-onset, lifelong","examples":["The lesson groups autism spectrum disorder and ADHD as neurodevelopmental disorders.","The chapter notes these disorders emerge early and persist across the life span."],"id":"ch14-lesson-036","simple":"neurodevelopmental disorders are typically diagnosed in childhood and continue through the life span","term":"Neurodevelopmental disorders","type":"lesson-concept"},{"apply":["In a group of 880 children, about how many would the chapter expect to have ASD?"],"category":"Childhood disorders","compare":"ASD: about 1 in 88, 5x more boys. ADHD: about 5% of children, 3x more boys.","cue":"1 in 88, boys 5x","examples":["The lesson gives the 1-in-88 estimate for autism spectrum disorder in the United States.","The chapter emphasizes the strong sex difference: five times more common in boys."],"id":"ch14-lesson-037","simple":"about 1 in 88 US children has ASD, and it is five times more common in boys than girls","term":"ASD prevalence and sex ratio","type":"lesson-concept"},{"apply":["A child avoids eye contact, plays alone, and does not engage in back-and-forth play. Which ASD feature is this?"],"category":"Childhood disorders","compare":"Social relatedness deficits affect connection with others; communication deficits affect language and speech.","cue":"social connection missing","examples":["The lesson says children with ASD do not make eye contact and prefer playing alone over playing with others.","The chapter notes they take no pleasure in reciprocal play and lack insight into another's point of view."],"id":"ch14-lesson-038","simple":"children with ASD show deficits in social relatedness, like avoiding eye contact and preferring to play alone","term":"ASD social relatedness deficits","type":"lesson-concept"},{"apply":["An 18-month-old produces no words and rarely babbles. Which ASD deficit does this show?"],"category":"Childhood disorders","compare":"Communication deficits are about language; repetitive behaviors are about rigid, repeated actions.","cue":"language lags or absent","examples":["The lesson lists communication deficits ranging from a total lack of speech to delayed language.","The chapter's quiz describes an 18-month-old who does not produce words and babbles infrequently."],"id":"ch14-lesson-039","simple":"ASD can involve a complete lack of speech or delayed language acquisition","term":"ASD communication deficits","type":"lesson-concept"},{"apply":["A child lines up toys in the exact same order every day and melts down if the order changes. Which ASD feature is this?"],"category":"Childhood disorders","compare":"Repetitive behaviors are rigid routines; social deficits are difficulties connecting with people.","cue":"same actions, again","examples":["The lesson says ASD is often accompanied by repetitive, ritualistic behaviors.","The chapter's quiz lists engaging in repetitive movements and highly restricted interests as an ASD characteristic."],"id":"ch14-lesson-040","simple":"ASD often includes repetitive, ritualistic behaviors and highly restricted interests","term":"ASD repetitive and ritualistic behaviors","type":"lesson-concept"},{"apply":["A school of 1,000 children would be expected to have about how many with ADHD, per the chapter?"],"category":"Childhood disorders","compare":"ADHD: about 5% of children, 3x more boys. ASD: about 1 in 88, 5x more boys.","cue":"5 percent, boys 3x","examples":["The lesson reports ADHD in approximately 5% of children.","The chapter notes it is three times more common in boys than in girls."],"id":"ch14-lesson-041","simple":"ADHD occurs in about 5% of children and is three times more common in boys than girls","term":"ADHD prevalence and sex ratio","type":"lesson-concept"},{"apply":["Maysoon rushes through tasks, taps her foot, and cannot sit still. Which ADHD component is most evident?"],"category":"Childhood disorders","compare":"Inattention is trouble staying focused; hyperactivity is excess movement; impulsivity is acting without thinking.","cue":"inattention, hyperactivity, impulsivity","examples":["The lesson defines inattention as inability to sustain attention and hyperactivity as high motor activity like fidgeting and squirming.","The chapter's quiz asks which term describes Maysoon's constant fidgeting and foot-tapping: hyperactivity."],"id":"ch14-lesson-042","simple":"ADHD involves unusual inattentiveness, hyperactivity, impulsivity, or a combination of these","term":"ADHD's three components","type":"lesson-concept"},{"apply":["An 18-month-old avoids eye contact and seems pained by hugs. Which disorder would the chapter's quiz point to?"],"category":"Childhood disorders","compare":"Early ASD signs center on missing social-communication milestones; ADHD signs center on attention and activity level.","cue":"Liam at 18 months","examples":["The chapter's quiz describes 18-month-old Liam: no words, infrequent babbling, no eye contact, distressed by hugs.","The lesson ties these early social and communication gaps to autism spectrum disorder."],"id":"ch14-lesson-043","simple":"early ASD signs include no words, rare babbling, no eye contact, and distress at being cuddled","term":"Early signs of ASD","type":"lesson-concept"},{"apply":["A friend says schizophrenia means having multiple personalities. How does the chapter correct this?"],"category":"Schizophrenia","compare":"Schizophrenia involves delusions and hallucinations; split personality (dissociative identity disorder) involves fragmented identity — a different disorder.","cue":"not multiple personalities","examples":["The lesson explicitly warns that schizophrenia is commonly mistaken for split personality but is unrelated to it.","The chapter stresses the mix-up even though the two conditions are completely different."],"id":"ch14-lesson-044","simple":"schizophrenia has nothing to do with dissociative identity disorder or so-called split personality","term":"Schizophrenia is not split personality","type":"lesson-concept"},{"apply":["In a city of 100,000 people, about how many would the chapter expect to develop schizophrenia?"],"category":"Schizophrenia","compare":"Schizophrenia: about 1%, early-adulthood onset. ASD: about 1 in 88, childhood onset.","cue":"1 percent, early adulthood","examples":["The lesson reports approximately 1% lifetime prevalence for schizophrenia.","The chapter notes individuals are typically diagnosed during early adulthood."],"id":"ch14-lesson-045","simple":"about 1% of people experience schizophrenia, typically diagnosed in early adulthood","term":"Schizophrenia onset and prevalence","type":"lesson-concept"},{"apply":["Joel hears voices telling him what to do but sees nothing unusual. Which hallucination type is this, and how common is it in schizophrenia?"],"category":"Schizophrenia","compare":"Auditory hallucinations are most common in schizophrenia; visual and olfactory hallucinations are less common.","cue":"hearing voices first","examples":["The lesson states auditory hallucinations are the most common in schizophrenia.","The chapter quiz's Joel hears voices commanding him — an auditory hallucination, not a visual one."],"id":"ch14-lesson-046","simple":"in schizophrenia, auditory hallucinations like hearing voices are the most common; visual and smell hallucinations are less common","term":"Auditory hallucinations are the most common","type":"lesson-concept"},{"apply":["A patient speaks in invented words no one else understands. Which sign of disorganized speech is this?"],"category":"Schizophrenia","compare":"Neologisms invent new words; rapid topic-shifting jumps between subjects — both signal disorganized speech.","cue":"made-up words","examples":["The lesson lists making up words as a sign of disorganized speech in schizophrenia.","The chapter groups neologisms with rapidly shifting topics and repeating words as disorganized speech."],"id":"ch14-lesson-047","simple":"neologisms are made-up words or phrases that have meaning only to the person with schizophrenia","term":"Neologisms","type":"lesson-concept"},{"apply":["A patient shows almost no facial expression and has stopped seeing friends or starting tasks. Which symptom group is this?"],"category":"Schizophrenia","compare":"Avolition and asociality take away motivation and social contact; delusions and hallucinations add false beliefs and perceptions.","cue":"motivation and affect gone","examples":["The lesson characterizes schizophrenia with avolition or asociality and restricted affect.","The chapter quiz contrasts avolition with delusions of paranoia as different symptom types."],"id":"ch14-lesson-048","simple":"schizophrenia can reduce motivation and social engagement through avolition, asociality, and restricted affect","term":"Negative symptoms of schizophrenia","type":"lesson-concept"},{"apply":["Someone is convinced their faithful partner is cheating despite zero evidence. Which delusion type is this?"],"category":"Schizophrenia","compare":"Jealousy delusions target a partner's fidelity; paranoid delusions target persecution by others.","cue":"false cheating belief","examples":["The chapter quiz feedback defines delusions of jealousy as unrealistic beliefs about a partner's infidelity.","The lesson's main delusion types are paranoia, grandiosity, and control — jealousy rounds out the set."],"id":"ch14-lesson-049","simple":"delusions of jealousy are unrealistic beliefs that one's partner is unfaithful","term":"Delusions of jealousy","type":"lesson-concept"},{"apply":["Joel hears voices ordering him to jump in front of a train. Which symptom is he experiencing?"],"category":"Schizophrenia","compare":"Command hallucinations order actions; other auditory hallucinations are voices without commands.","cue":"voices giving orders","examples":["The chapter quiz's Joel hears voices commanding him to commit suicide and struggles to resist them.","The lesson notes hallucinations are false perceptions with no real stimulus — commands make them especially dangerous."],"id":"ch14-lesson-050","simple":"command hallucinations are voices that order the person to act, sometimes in dangerous ways","term":"Command hallucinations","type":"lesson-concept"},{"apply":["A student claims schizophrenia is purely genetic. How does the chapter's causal account correct this?"],"category":"Schizophrenia","compare":"Biological factors create vulnerability; environmental factors like extreme stress help trigger the disorder.","cue":"genes plus stress","examples":["The lesson says the causes are not fully known but include genetic, neurological, and environmental contributors.","The chapter names extreme stress as an environmental factor in schizophrenia's development."],"id":"ch14-lesson-051","simple":"schizophrenia arises from biological factors like genetics and neurology plus environmental factors like extreme stress","term":"Biological and environmental causes of schizophrenia","type":"lesson-concept"},{"apply":["A student has a manic episode but has never been depressed. Can this still fit bipolar disorder? Why?"],"category":"Mood disorders","compare":"Mania is the elevated-mood phase; depression is the low phase — bipolar disorder can involve one without the other.","cue":"Think: mania can stand alone","examples":["The lesson states that mania may or may not be followed by a period of depression.","Mariel shows mania — rapid speech, no sleep, reckless spending — with no mention of a depressive phase."],"id":"ch14-lesson-052","simple":"In bipolar disorder, a manic episode does not always come with a depressive episode.","term":"Mania may or may not be followed by depression","type":"lesson-concept"},{"apply":["Mariel talks rapidly, hasn't slept, spent $5,000, and is quitting school for an unrealistic career. Which characteristic of bipolar disorder is this?"],"category":"Mood disorders","compare":"Mania is elevated, overactive mood; depression is the opposite pole of the same disorder.","cue":"Think: no sleep, big spending, grand plans","examples":["The lesson's Mariel talks rapidly, stays up all night, spent $5,000 on clothes and shoes, and plans to become a professional chef though she has never cooked.","The lesson notes people in mania may be too active to eat or sleep and grow irritated with anyone who questions their plans."],"id":"ch14-lesson-053","simple":"Mania shows up as unrealistically elevated mood with too much energy to eat or sleep and impulsive, unrealistic plans.","term":"Recognizing mania: the Mariel vignette","type":"lesson-concept"},{"apply":["Why might bipolar disorder and schizophrenia sometimes run in the same families?"],"category":"Mood disorders","compare":"Shared genes raise risk for both, but mania defines bipolar disorder while it does not define schizophrenia.","cue":"Think: shared genes, different disorders","examples":["The lesson reports considerable overlap in the genes thought to underlie both bipolar disorder and schizophrenia.","The lesson also notes a family history or genetic predisposition raises a person's likelihood of developing bipolar disorder."],"id":"ch14-lesson-054","simple":"Many of the same genes are believed to be involved in both bipolar disorder and schizophrenia.","term":"Gene overlap between bipolar disorder and schizophrenia","type":"lesson-concept"},{"apply":["A client has bipolar disorder and also meets criteria for an anxiety disorder. What term describes having both?"],"category":"Mood disorders","compare":"Comorbidity means co-occurring disorders; a misdiagnosis means the wrong disorder was named.","cue":"Think: bipolar plus anxiety is common","examples":["The lesson states that individuals with bipolar disorder often also have an anxiety disorder.","This is an example of comorbidity — two disorders occurring together in one person."],"id":"ch14-lesson-055","simple":"People with bipolar disorder often also have an anxiety disorder.","term":"Bipolar disorder often co-occurs with anxiety","type":"lesson-concept"},{"apply":["A patient has five depressive symptoms for three weeks but still enjoys all her usual hobbies. Can she be diagnosed with MDD? Why not?"],"category":"Mood disorders","compare":"Anhedonia is loss of pleasure; depressed mood is feeling sad — an MDD diagnosis requires at least one of the two.","cue":"Think: no pleasure in old favorites","examples":["The lesson defines anhedonia as disinterest in activities that previously provided pleasure.","To diagnose MDD, a person must show at least five symptoms for two weeks, and anhedonia must be one of them."],"id":"ch14-lesson-056","simple":"Anhedonia is a loss of interest in activities that used to bring pleasure, and it is a required symptom for an MDD diagnosis.","term":"Anhedonia","type":"lesson-concept"},{"apply":["Why does the lesson require MDD symptoms to be a change from previous functioning?"],"category":"Mood disorders","compare":"A change from baseline signals a disorder; a lifelong trait is just how the person has always been.","cue":"Think: a real shift, not just a bad week","examples":["The lesson requires that MDD symptoms represent a change in the person's previous level of functioning.","Someone who has always slept little is different from someone whose sleep suddenly collapses for weeks."],"id":"ch14-lesson-057","simple":"For an MDD diagnosis, the symptoms must represent a change from how the person used to function.","term":"MDD must be a change from previous functioning","type":"lesson-concept"},{"apply":["True or false: patients with bipolar disorder have low levels of omega-3 fatty acids compared to healthy controls."],"category":"Mood disorders","compare":"Omega-3 levels are a biological finding; mania is the defining behavioral symptom.","cue":"Think: low omega-3, bipolar link","examples":["The chapter quiz reports that bipolar patients show low omega-3 levels compared to healthy controls.","This is a biological correlate of bipolar disorder, alongside genetic and neurological factors."],"id":"ch14-lesson-058","simple":"Patients with bipolar disorder have lower levels of omega-3 fatty acids than healthy controls.","term":"Omega-3 fatty acids and bipolar disorder","type":"lesson-concept"},{"apply":["In a lecture hall of 200 students, about how many would the lesson predict will meet criteria for an anxiety disorder in their lifetime?"],"category":"Anxiety, trauma, and somatic disorders","compare":"Lifetime prevalence counts anyone who ever qualifies; yearly prevalence, like PTSD's 3–4%, counts only the past year.","cue":"Think: one in four will qualify","examples":["The lesson reports that roughly 25% to 30% of Americans qualify for an anxiety disorder at some point.","That makes anxiety disorders among the most common psychological disorders."],"id":"ch14-lesson-059","simple":"About 25% to 30% of the U.S. population will meet the criteria for an anxiety disorder in their lifetime.","term":"Lifetime prevalence of anxiety disorders","type":"lesson-concept"},{"apply":["How does the lesson distinguish normal anxiety from an anxiety disorder?"],"category":"Anxiety, trauma, and somatic disorders","compare":"Normal anxiety is proportional and protective; disordered anxiety is disproportionate and impairing.","cue":"Think: useful alarm turned up too high","examples":["The lesson explains that normal anxiety protects us from engaging in risky activities.","The disorder version is anxiety that is not proportional to the circumstances — it makes people withdraw from positive experiences."],"id":"ch14-lesson-060","simple":"Anxiety disorders are an exaggeration of a normal, useful response to danger.","term":"Anxiety as an exaggerated normal response","type":"lesson-concept"},{"apply":["Katarzyna has 10 minutes of pounding heart, sweating, trembling, and shortness of breath. What did her doctor conclude she experienced?"],"category":"Anxiety, trauma, and somatic disorders","compare":"A panic attack is one episode; panic disorder is repeated attacks plus fear of future attacks.","cue":"Think: 10 minutes of terror, then gone","examples":["The chapter quiz describes Katarzyna: pounding heart, sweating, trembling, shortness of breath, chest pain, nausea, and dizziness for about 10 minutes.","Her doctor identified it as a panic attack — a single episode, not yet a disorder."],"id":"ch14-lesson-061","simple":"A panic attack is a sudden burst of intense fear with pounding heart, sweating, trembling, and shortness of breath.","term":"Recognizing a panic attack: the Katarzyna vignette","type":"lesson-concept"},{"apply":["Maxime feels extremely worried for no clear reason and cannot relax even though life is going well. Which condition fits best?"],"category":"Anxiety, trauma, and somatic disorders","compare":"GAD is free-floating worry about nothing in particular; a specific phobia is fear tied to one object or situation.","cue":"Think: worried for no reason, can't relax","examples":["The chapter quiz describes Maxime: extremely worried and anxious for reasons he cannot explain, unable to relax though things are going well.","The lesson notes GAD involves excessive worry not tied to particular objects or situations."],"id":"ch14-lesson-062","simple":"Generalized anxiety disorder is excessive, hard-to-explain worry that persists even when things are going well.","term":"Recognizing GAD: the Maxime vignette","type":"lesson-concept"},{"apply":["Which disorders does the DSM-5 group together with obsessive-compulsive disorder?"],"category":"Anxiety, trauma, and somatic disorders","compare":"The OCD group shares intrusive thoughts and repetitive behaviors; anxiety disorders share disproportionate fear and tension.","cue":"Think: OCD has a family","examples":["The lesson lists OCD, body dysmorphic disorder, and hoarding disorder as a DSM-5 group.","Each affects between 2% and 5% of the U.S. population."],"id":"ch14-lesson-063","simple":"The DSM-5 groups OCD with body dysmorphic disorder, hoarding disorder, and similar disorders.","term":"DSM-5 groups OCD-related disorders together","type":"lesson-concept"},{"id":"ch14-lesson-064","term":"What OCD-group disorders share","type":"lesson-concept","category":"Anxiety, trauma, and somatic disorders","cue":"Think: stuck thoughts, repeated acts","simple":"The OCD-related disorders all generally involve intrusive, unwanted thoughts and repetitive behaviors.","examples":["The lesson reviews these disorders together because they share intrusive thoughts and repetitive behaviors.","Shirlee’s repeated door-locking checks show the repetitive-behavior side of the pattern."],"apply":["What common feature justifies grouping OCD, body dysmorphic disorder, and hoarding disorder together?"],"compare":"OCD-group disorders center on intrusive thoughts and rituals; dissociative disorders center on disruptions of identity and memory."},{"id":"ch14-lesson-065","term":"PTSD’s single cause: trauma","type":"lesson-concept","category":"Anxiety, trauma, and somatic disorders","cue":"Think: one disorder, one cause","simple":"PTSD is unique in the DSM-5 because it has a single cause: experiencing trauma.","examples":["The lesson states PTSD is unique among DSM-5 disorders in having a single cause — the experience of trauma.","About 3% to 4% of U.S. adults experience PTSD each year."],"apply":["What makes PTSD unique among the disorders listed in the DSM-5?"],"compare":"PTSD requires a trauma; most disorders, like bipolar disorder or MDD, have multiple contributing causes."},{"id":"ch14-lesson-066","term":"PTSD yearly prevalence","type":"lesson-concept","category":"Anxiety, trauma, and somatic disorders","cue":"Think: 3 to 4 percent each year","simple":"Approximately 3% to 4% of U.S. adults experience PTSD each year.","examples":["The lesson reports that 3% to 4% of the adult U.S. population experiences PTSD each year.","That yearly rate is far lower than the 25–30% lifetime rate for anxiety disorders overall."],"apply":["In a town of 10,000 adults, about how many would the lesson predict experience PTSD in a given year?"],"compare":"PTSD’s 3–4% is a yearly rate; anxiety disorders’ 25–30% is a lifetime rate."},{"id":"ch14-lesson-067","term":"PTSD symptom trio: hypervigilance, numbing, reexperiencing","type":"lesson-concept","category":"Anxiety, trauma, and somatic disorders","cue":"Think: on guard, shut down, reliving","simple":"PTSD can show as hypervigilance, emotional numbing, and reexperiencing the trauma.","examples":["The chapter quiz describes Pietro, two years after Hurricane Michael: hypervigilant, emotionally numb, and reexperiencing the storm every time it rains.","These three — staying on guard, shutting down feelings, and reliving the event — are classic PTSD signs."],"apply":["Two years after a hurricane, Pietro is hypervigilant, emotionally numb, and relives the storm when it rains. Which disorder fits?"],"compare":"PTSD symptoms follow a trauma; GAD worry has no single triggering event."},{"id":"ch14-lesson-068","term":"Dissociative amnesia is event-specific","type":"lesson-concept","category":"Anxiety, trauma, and somatic disorders","cue":"Think: forgot the robbery, kept the rest","simple":"In dissociative amnesia, the person cannot recall one frightening event but remembers everything else.","examples":["The lesson’s example: a crime victim has no memory of being robbed at gunpoint but recalls other details from the same day.","The memory loss usually follows a frightening or traumatic event."],"apply":["A robbery victim cannot remember the robbery but recalls everything else from that day. Which disorder fits?"],"compare":"Dissociative amnesia is lost memory of an event; dissociative fugue is lost identity plus travel."},{"id":"ch14-lesson-069","term":"DID is controversial and rare","type":"lesson-concept","category":"Anxiety, trauma, and somatic disorders","cue":"Think: real cases are very rare","simple":"Dissociative identity disorder — two or more distinct personality states — is controversial, and true cases are very rare.","examples":["The lesson calls DID a controversial disorder and notes true cases are very rare.","The lesson’s Kim Noble example shows distinct states like destructive ‘Julie’ and calm ‘Patricia’ emerging after childhood abuse."],"apply":["Why does the lesson describe dissociative identity disorder as controversial?"],"compare":"DID involves multiple personality states; depersonalization is a feeling of unreality about the self."},{"id":"ch14-lesson-070","term":"Somatic symptom disorder usually involves multiple symptoms","type":"lesson-concept","category":"Anxiety, trauma, and somatic disorders","cue":"Think: many symptoms, no medical cause","simple":"Most people with somatic symptom disorder have multiple physical symptoms with no underlying medical cause, though some have just one severe symptom like pain.","examples":["The chapter quiz notes most diagnosed individuals have multiple somatic symptoms.","The single-symptom cases are typically severe pain that may not be fully explained by another diagnosis."],"apply":["A patient has fatigue, shortness of breath, and pain, but no medical cause is found. Which disorder category fits?"],"compare":"Somatic symptom disorders are unexplained physical symptoms; dissociative disorders are disruptions of identity, memory, or consciousness."},{"id":"ch14-lesson-071","term":"What defines a personality disorder","type":"lesson-concept","category":"Personality disorders","cue":"Think: identity, traits, connection","simple":"A personality disorder means lasting impairments in identity, personality traits, and the ability to feel empathy or build intimacy.","examples":["The lesson defines personality disorders by impairments in identity, traits, empathy, and intimacy.","These are long-standing patterns, not temporary episodes like mania or depression."],"apply":["What three areas are impaired in a personality disorder, according to the lesson?"],"compare":"Personality disorders are enduring traits; mood disorders are episodic shifts in mood and energy."},{"id":"ch14-lesson-072","term":"Three clusters of personality disorders","type":"lesson-concept","category":"Personality disorders","cue":"Think: three groups, similar symptoms","simple":"Personality disorders are grouped into three clusters based on similar characteristics and symptoms.","examples":["The lesson explains the clustering is by similar characteristics and symptoms.","The lesson focuses on three: antisocial, borderline, and narcissistic personality disorders."],"apply":["On what basis are personality disorders grouped into three clusters?"],"compare":"Clusters group disorders by similar traits; comorbidity describes one person having two different disorders."},{"id":"ch14-lesson-073","term":"Personality disorder prevalence","type":"lesson-concept","category":"Personality disorders","cue":"Think: about one in ten","simple":"Studies report that 9% to 15% of the population has at least one personality disorder.","examples":["The lesson cites 9% to 15% of the general population meeting criteria for at least one personality disorder.","That makes them more common than most students expect."],"apply":["About what share of the population has at least one personality disorder, according to the lesson?"],"compare":"Personality disorders (9–15%) are less common than lifetime anxiety disorders (25–30%) but more common than yearly PTSD (3–4%)."},{"id":"ch14-lesson-074","term":"Recognizing ASPD: the Kuklinski example","type":"lesson-concept","category":"Personality disorders","cue":"Think: no remorse, no rules","simple":"Antisocial personality disorder shows as a lack of remorse and empathy plus disregard for normal social rules.","examples":["The chapter quiz describes hitman Richard Kuklinski: little emotional response and little regard for social rules and conventions.","The lesson adds the pattern: lying, fighting, trouble with the law, irresponsibility, and no remorse."],"apply":["Kuklinski showed little emotional response and disregarded normal social rules. Which personality disorder was he diagnosed with?"],"compare":"ASPD is cold disregard for others’ rights; narcissistic personality disorder is grandiosity and need for admiration."},{"id":"ch14-lesson-075","term":"Personality disorders beyond the big three","type":"lesson-concept","category":"Personality disorders","cue":"Think: schizotypal, avoidant, histrionic","simple":"Beyond antisocial, borderline, and narcissistic, the lesson names schizotypal, avoidant, and histrionic personality disorders.","examples":["The lesson lists schizotypal, avoidant, and histrionic as other recognized personality disorders.","The lesson’s main focus stays on ASPD, borderline, and narcissistic types."],"apply":["Name three personality disorders other than antisocial, borderline, and narcissistic."],"compare":"The big three get the lesson’s focus; the others are recognized but covered only by name."}];